Provenance of the NDS machine layer (runtime/guests/nds)

Most of this layer is fresh C written from GBATEK (problemkaputt.de/gbatek.htm:
"DS Memory Maps", "DS Memory Control", "DS Memory Timings", "DS Interrupts",
"DS Inter Process Communication (IPC)", "DS Timers", "DS Maths", "ARM CP15
Tightly Coupled Memory"): mem.c, bus.c, io.c, irq.c, ipc.c, math.c. The
ordering of the two CPUs (order.c) follows relift's spike 20-nds q3.

The high-level BIOS (bios.c, bios_copy.c, bios_decomp.c, bios_tables.c) is fresh C
from GBATEK ("BIOS Function Summary" and the sections it lists, "DS Memory
Control - BIOS"). Its algorithms follow relift's GBA BIOS (runtime/guests/gba/
bios), which the DS routines share. No BIOS image, code or table is used. The
sine and pitch tables are the formulas in bios_tables.c; the volume table is a
fit of GBATEK's stated range to the values an open BIOS replacement returns
when run as a black box (its source was not read). Results were checked
against that replacement as a black box (tests/bios_oracle.h) and, where it
disagrees with GBATEK, GBATEK is followed (test_bios.c names each case).

ares has no NDS core. The DS timers are the GBA's timers clocked by the DS bus
(GBATEK "DS Timers"), so timer.c is relift's GBA timer (runtime/guests/gba/
timer.c) for two CPUs with bus-cycle counters. That file was ported from ares,
https://github.com/ares-emulator/ares, commit
0b928930af4cc59ed99e942996f0d3c815379388, ISC license (text below), and
timer.c keeps the ISC notice in its header.

timer.c   prescalers, cascade, overflow, the control and reload latches
                                   gba/cpu/timer.cpp, io.cpp, cpu.cpp

